  { q: 'Why does a cup of flour weigh less than a cup of sugar?', a: 'Because flour is less dense than sugar, so the same volume holds less weight. King Arthur puts a cup of all-purpose flour at 120 grams and a cup of granulated sugar at 198 grams. That is why a single cups-to-grams number is wrong for most ingredients, and why the Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter asks for the ingredient first.' },
  { q: 'How many grams is a cup of all-purpose flour?', a: 'King Arthur Baking, the reference bakers cite, puts one US cup of all-purpose flour at 120 grams, though many recipes assume closer to 125 and a scooped cup can reach 140. The spread is why weighing beats scooping. The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter uses real densities so you can switch flour from cups to grams.' },
