      <article>
        <p class="lead"><strong>A cup measures volume and a gram measures weight, so converting between them depends entirely on the ingredient: a cup of all-purpose flour is about 120 grams, a cup of granulated sugar about 198 grams, and a cup of water about 227 grams.</strong> The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter uses the real density of each ingredient, and it lets you pick a US or a metric cup, so the answer matches your recipe. This guide covers why the ingredient matters, how many grams are in a cup, the different cup standards, the tablespoon gotcha, and oven temperatures.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/cooking-converter/">Cooking Converter</a> is a single-page tool with two modes: an ingredient converter that moves between volume and weight using each ingredient's real density, so a cup of flour and a cup of sugar give different gram weights, and a recipe scaler that adjusts a recipe up or down and returns kitchen-friendly fractions like two-thirds and one and a half rather than awkward decimals. It runs entirely in your browser, so nothing is uploaded, it needs no account, shows no ads, and keeps working offline, which is handy on a phone at the counter. The converter sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/">Calculators hub</a> and the wider <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

        <div class="cav">The core idea</div>
        <h2 id="density">Why does a cup of flour weigh less than a cup of sugar?</h2>
        <p>Because they have different densities, so the same volume holds a different weight. A cup is a fixed amount of space; how much that space weighs depends on what fills it. King Arthur Baking's Ingredient Weight Chart, the reference bakers cite, puts one cup of all-purpose flour at 120 grams, one cup of granulated sugar at 198 grams, and one cup of water at 227 grams (<a href="https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/learn/ingredient-weight-chart" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">King Arthur Baking</a>). Same cup, three different weights. This is why a single "one cup equals X grams" rule is wrong for most ingredients, and why a converter has to know what you are measuring. The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter asks for the ingredient first, then applies its real density.</p>
        <figure class="chart">
