        <div class="cav">The numbers</div>
        <h2 id="weights">How many grams are in a cup?</h2>
        <p>It depends on the ingredient, and the table below gives the common baking staples from King Arthur's chart. Note the flour figure: King Arthur uses 120 grams per cup, though many US recipes assume closer to 125, and a scooped cup can hit 140 or more once the flour packs down. That spread is exactly why weighing beats measuring by volume, and King Arthur says so directly: <a href="https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/learn/ingredient-weight-chart" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"For best results, we recommend weighing your ingredients with a digital scale."</a> The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter carries these densities so you can switch a recipe from cups to grams in a tap.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Ingredient</th><th>1 US cup</th><th>In ounces</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">All-purpose flour</td><td>120 g</td><td>4 1/4 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Granulated sugar</td><td>198 g</td><td>7 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Brown sugar (packed)</td><td>213 g</td><td>7 1/2 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Butter</td><td>about 227 g (16 tbsp)</td><td>8 oz</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Water</td><td>227 g</td><td>8 oz</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <blockquote>For best results, we recommend <span class="accent">weighing your ingredients with a digital scale.</span><cite>King Arthur Baking, Ingredient Weight Chart</cite></blockquote>

        <div class="cav">The cup question</div>
        <h2 id="cups">Whose cup is it, US or metric?</h2>
        <p>A "cup" is not one size, and mixing them up shifts a recipe by up to about six percent. The US customary cup used by most American measuring cups is 236.588 milliliters, but the "legal" cup on US nutrition labels is rounded to 240 milliliters, which the FDA fixes in regulation: <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-101/subpart-A/section-101.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">"a cup means 240 milliliters."</a> The metric cup used across Australia, New Zealand, and much of the Commonwealth is 250 milliliters, and the older imperial cup was about 284 milliliters (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cup_(unit)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cup unit</a>). The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter lets you choose the cup standard so the grams match the recipe's origin.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Cup type</th><th>Volume</th><th>Where it is used</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">US customary</td><td>236.6 ml</td><td>US measuring cups and recipes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">US legal (label)</td><td>240 ml</td><td>US nutrition labels</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Metric</td><td>250 ml</td><td>Australia, NZ, much of the Commonwealth</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Imperial (historical)</td><td>about 284 ml</td><td>Older UK recipes, now rare</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="cav">The spoon trap</div>
        <h2 id="spoons">Tablespoons and teaspoons</h2>
        <p>A US tablespoon is three teaspoons, about 14.79 milliliters, and a US teaspoon is about 4.93 milliliters (<a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/unit-conversion/approximate-conversions-us-customary-measures-metric" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST</a>). The international metric tablespoon used on labels and in most metric recipes is exactly 15 milliliters. The one that catches people out is the Australian tablespoon, which is 20 milliliters, or four teaspoons rather than three (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tablespoon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tablespoon</a>). So an Australian recipe's tablespoon is a third larger than a US or metric one, which matters for potent ingredients like baking soda or salt. The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter handles the spoon sizes so you do not over- or under-measure.</p>

        <div class="cav">The oven dial</div>
        <h2 id="oven">How do you convert oven temperatures?</h2>
        <p>Convert Fahrenheit to Celsius with C equals F minus 32, times five, divided by nine. The catch is rounding: 350 degrees Fahrenheit is exactly 176.7 Celsius, but oven dials and European recipes usually round it to 180, so a converter should be clear about which it is showing. The table below lines up the common baking temperatures with their gas marks. One more note for fan or convection ovens: they typically run about 20 degrees Celsius cooler than the conventional setting, so drop the temperature accordingly. The Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter shows the exact conversion and the usual dial value together.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Fahrenheit</th><th>Celsius</th><th>Gas mark</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">325&deg;F</td><td>165&deg;C</td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">350&deg;F</td><td>180&deg;C (177 exact)</td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">375&deg;F</td><td>190&deg;C</td><td>5</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">400&deg;F</td><td>205&deg;C</td><td>6</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">425&deg;F</td><td>220&deg;C</td><td>7</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">The Australian tablespoon, and the fan oven</div><p>Two gotchas worth a second look: an Australian tablespoon is 20 ml, a third bigger than the 15 ml used elsewhere, so watch it in salt, baking soda, and spice. And a fan or convection oven runs about 20 Celsius cooler than a conventional one at the same dial, so lower the temperature or shorten the time.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from other converters?</h2>
        <p>The difference is that the Best Answer Hub Cooking Converter is ingredient-aware and explicit about the cup standard, where many converters apply one generic density and quietly assume a US cup. Some good ones do handle density, so the wedge is the full package: per-ingredient weights, a US or metric cup choice, oven temperatures, and a recipe scaler, all free of ads and signups, and working offline in a kitchen. The table sets the usual experience next to this one.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Best Answer Hub</th><th>Typical converter</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Ingredient-aware cups to grams</td><td class="band">Yes, pick the ingredient</td><td>Often one generic density</td></tr>
            <tr><td>US vs metric cup clarity</td><td class="band">You choose</td><td>Usually assumes US, unstated</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tablespoon size differences</td><td class="band">Handled</td><td>Rarely mentioned</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Oven temperatures</td><td class="band">Included</td><td>Often a separate tool</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No ads, no signup</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>Often ad-supported</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Works offline</td><td class="band">Yes</td><td>Usually not</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">Weigh when it counts</div><p>For baking, where ratios matter, a digital scale and grams beat cups every time, because a scooped cup of flour can vary by 20 percent or more. Use the converter to turn a cup recipe into grams once, then weigh from then on. For everyday cooking, the cup and spoon conversions are plenty.</p></div>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">Pair it with the rest of the hub</div><p>The Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/">Calculators hub</a> keeps the other everyday converters close, from a <a href="/calculators/unit-currency-converter/">Unit and Currency Converter</a> to a <a href="/calculators/data-storage-converter/">Data Storage Converter</a>, each running in the browser and sending nothing.</p></div>

